window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-b70d1395449a00f1"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"hipaa-configuration-guide-for-codex\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"codex-hipaa\">Codex HIPAA 配置指南</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取文档页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"whos-this-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">适用对象</h2> <p>本指南面向 IT 管理员和合规专业人员，介绍在 Codex 中管理受保护健康信息（PHI）的共同责任。Codex 包括运行在用户计算机上的 ChatGPT 桌面应用、Codex IDE 扩展和 Codex CLI；本指南不涵盖在云端使用 Codex。</p> <p>如果你使用 ChatGPT for Healthcare、ChatGPT for Clinicians 或受监管工作区，拥有适用的 OpenAI Business Associate Agreement（BAA），并已获得所需的 Codex 访问权限，则 OpenAI 会依据该 BAA 处理从 Codex 收到的 PHI。OpenAI 会安全地处理你使用 Codex 时提交的提示、文件和其他输入，并安全地向你返回输出。</p> <p>OpenAI 与你的组织共同负责保障 OpenAI 服务的安全。你负责安全地配置本地工作站、源代码库、本地数据保留、本地 MCP 服务器、Browser Use 和 Computer Use 活动、桌面应用，以及插件和应用等第三方服务。</p> <p>OpenAI 提供了帮助你配置 Codex 的资源，包括<a href=\"https://openai.com/index/running-codex-safely/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">网页（需联网）</a>、<a href=\"#/article/a-f05fc21d24fe24c1\">文档</a>，以及 Trust Portal 上的 <a href=\"https://trust.openai.com/?itemUid=382f924d-54f3-43a8-a9df-c39e6c959958&amp;source=click\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex 安全白皮书（需联网）</a>。允许 Codex 处理 PHI 前，请先审阅这些资料。本指南提供了一些工具用法示例。</p> <p>本指南不构成法律意见。如果你属于 HIPAA 所称的受监管实体（covered entity）或业务伙伴（business associate），请依据 HIPAA 策略、风险分析、BAA 清单、端点控制和数据流文档审查最终配置。</p> <p><a id=\"shared-responsibility\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">共同责任</h2> <p>与多数云解决方案一样，云服务提供商和客户共同承担合规责任。ChatGPT Enterprise 会在 OpenAI 云端存储输入和输出。用户的工作站也会保留 Codex 的输入和输出。Codex 会将提示和文件等输入发送给 OpenAI 进行推理，再由 OpenAI 返回输出。通过 ChatGPT 进行身份验证的使用记录最多保留 30 天，你可以通过 <a href=\"#/article/a-05f8d80ccc7b0b64?anchor=compliance-api\">Compliance API</a> 检索这些记录。OpenAI 不会使用 ChatGPT Enterprise 或 Codex 数据训练模型。</p> <p>本地工作站配置（尤其是 TOML 策略文件）决定 Codex 在用户计算机上可以执行哪些操作。它会影响 Codex 是否可以读写文件、运行命令、访问网络、调用插件或连接器及 MCP 工具、打开浏览器界面，以及保留本地会话记录。</p> <p>这些设置不会改变 OpenAI 在 BAA 下的义务，但它们是 HIPAA 保障措施的核心。你有责任根据 PHI 处理策略配置这些设置。</p> <p><a id=\"openai-security-program\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"openai\">OpenAI 安全计划</h2> <p>OpenAI 维护企业安全计划，旨在保护 OpenAI 服务处理的数据，并帮助受监管组织履行合规义务。有关 OpenAI 安全计划的更多信息，请参阅 <a href=\"https://trust.openai.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Trust Portal（需联网）</a>。</p> <p>OpenAI 实施 <strong>企业风险管理</strong>计划，并建立正式的风险治理架构，包括向董事会委员会报告。产品保障工作有助于确保产品发布保留加密、最小权限访问和细粒度日志等保护措施，以支持 HIPAA 合规。产品风险评估、控制措施监控和合规审查有助于识别可合理预见的数据风险、评估保护措施的有效性，并持续改进 ChatGPT Enterprise、API 平台和 Codex 相关服务所采用的控制措施。</p> <p><strong>安全开发与 CI/CD 保障措施</strong>有助于降低 Codex 相关服务的变更引入未授权访问、数据泄露或完整性问题的风险。这些措施包括受控的源代码访问、同行评审、自动化测试、构建与部署流程中的安全检查、机密信息处理控制以及受监控的部署流程。受控的软件交付流程用于保障 OpenAI 服务层；但你仍需负责本地代码库的整洁、工作站安全，以及本地策略文件配置所允许的行为。</p> <p><strong>OpenAI 的漏洞管理计划</strong>包括持续扫描、依赖项和基础设施审查、按严重程度分级处理、修复跟踪以及修复验证。OpenAI 还会通过内部和外部红队、独立安全测试及负责任披露渠道，在安全弱点影响你的数据之前发现并处理这些问题。</p> <p><strong>数据保护控制措施</strong>包括对传输中和静态存储的数据进行加密、身份与访问控制、基于角色的管理、日志记录，以及遵循适用 ChatGPT Enterprise 或 API 组织设置的数据保留控制。</p> <p><a id=\"codex-sign-in\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"codex\">Codex 登录方式</h2> <p>使用 OpenAI 模型时，Codex 支持两种 OpenAI 登录方式：用于订阅访问的 ChatGPT 登录，以及按用量计费的 API 密钥登录。ChatGPT 登录要求账号符合 HIPAA 资格、适用的 OpenAI BAA 已生效，并且已配置所需的 Codex 访问权限和工作区权限。使用 API 密钥登录时，只有当 BAA 将启用修改后数据保留的 API 服务列为合格服务时，BAA 才涵盖由 OpenAI 处理的数据。除非另有说明，OpenAI 还必须为该 API 组织配置修改后数据保留。详情请参阅 <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001069-hipaa-eligible-products-and-functionality\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HIPAA 合格产品与功能（需联网）</a>。对于任何已安装插件或应用中可能访问 PHI 的第三方，你也有责任与其签订 BAA。</p> <p>使用 ChatGPT 登录时，Codex 会遵循用户的 ChatGPT 工作区权限、基于角色的访问控制（RBAC），以及 ChatGPT Enterprise 的数据保留和驻留设置。使用 API 密钥登录时，Codex 则遵循 OpenAI API 组织的数据保留、数据共享和管理设置，而不是 ChatGPT 工作区设置。API 密钥登录常用于以编程方式运行 Codex CLI 的工作流，例如受信任的 CI/CD 作业；但不要在公开或不受信任的执行环境中暴露 API 密钥。</p> <p><a id=\"your-responsibilities\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">你的责任</h2> <p>你仍需对运行 Codex 的工作站负责。请自行完成本地使用 Codex 的风险分析，并考虑工作站配置、操作系统安全、磁盘加密、恶意软件防护、设备管理、补丁更新、用户访问、安全凭据存储和本地数据保留等控制措施。</p> <p>你需要决定哪些用户可以使用 Codex、他们可以使用哪些登录方式和访问哪些工作区、是否允许通过 API 密钥登录、哪些代码库和文件夹可以包含 PHI，以及 Codex 是否可以使用外部服务。</p> <p>你还需对已启用的第三方服务及有权访问这些服务的用户负责。如果组织在含有 PHI 的环境中启用了 Microsoft SharePoint、Google Drive、GitHub 或其他服务的浏览器目标、插件、连接器或 MCP 服务器，请确认组织批准该服务用于处理 PHI，并已签订适当的 BAA 或同等的医疗保健附加协议。OpenAI 的 BAA 不会使其他供应商自动成为符合 HIPAA 要求的数据接收方。</p> <p>以下各节将说明如何使用 <code>requirements.toml</code> 策略配置文件及相关设置来控制 Codex 的行为。其他设置请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-f05fc21d24fe24c1\">OpenAI 文档</a>；随着 Codex 功能变化，请定期重新审查这些设置。</p> <p><a id=\"enable-codex\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"codex_1\">启用 Codex</h2> <p>按照 <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise 管理员设置说明</a>为工作区启用 Codex Local，并确认用户拥有所需权限且你已与 OpenAI 签订 BAA。</p> <p><strong>BAA 不涵盖 Codex 云端服务。请勿在 Codex 云端服务中处理 PHI。</strong></p> <p><a id=\"configure-role-based-access-control\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">配置基于角色的访问控制</h2> <p>你可以使用 RBAC 自定义 Codex 的访问权限和配置。例如，不处理 PHI 的用户可以使用较宽松的配置，而处理 PHI 的用户则可使用本指南中的配置。请在 <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/permissions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT 管理员权限与角色页面（需联网）</a>控制整个组织对 Codex 的访问。若要管理特定用户的访问权限，请创建用户组并编辑这些组的权限。</p> <p><a id=\"review-apps-and-plugins\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"review-plugins-and-connectors\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">审查插件和连接器</h2> <p>ChatGPT 桌面应用中的 Codex 和 Codex CLI 支持插件；插件可包含连接器和技能。IDE 扩展不支持插件。连接器允许你与第三方数据源交换数据。启用带有连接器的插件前，请确认是否需要与通过该连接器接收数据的任何第三方签订 BAA。技能是在策略配置范围内运行的指令。请像审查其他脚本一样，确认技能适合预期用途。</p> <p>工作区管理员必须先通过插件控制将插件设为可用，并单独启用其中的连接器，用户才能使用。请在<a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">连接器设置（需联网）</a>中配置连接器访问权限。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-managed-requirements-and-defaults\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">配置托管要求与默认设置</h2> <p>TOML 配置文件中的要求和托管默认设置用于控制 Codex 的行为。若要设置用户无法覆盖的管理员强制约束，请在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中使用托管要求。OpenAI 建议使用托管配置强制执行 PHI 数据处理要求。</p> <p>管理员可在 <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/codex/settings/managed-configs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex 托管配置页面（需联网）</a>使用兼容 <code>requirements.toml</code> 的语法配置云端托管要求，也可通过 macOS MDM 等设备管理方式分发要求。Codex 按优先级从低到高应用要求：系统级 <code>requirements.toml</code>、云端托管要求、旧版 <code>managed_config.toml</code> 要求，以及 macOS MDM 要求。高优先级层会覆盖普通标量值和列表值；部分要求则按字段采用特定的合并行为。</p> <p>若要将 PHI 工作流限制在获准的 ChatGPT 工作区，请通过系统级 <code>requirements.toml</code> 或 MDM 同时部署 <code>allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]</code> 和 <code>allowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"&lt;workspace-id&gt;\"]</code>。云端托管要求会忽略这两项设置，而且仅限制工作区并不能阻止用户使用 API 密钥登录。API 密钥工作流同样需要系统级或 MDM 要求，因为它们不会接收工作区的云端托管要求。</p> <p>托管默认设置与要求是分开的。它们设定 Codex 启动时使用的初始配置，但用户可在会话期间更改这些设置；Codex 下次启动时会重新应用默认值。托管默认设置用于标准化，不用于严格的合规强制执行。例如，你可以设置默认模型、权限配置文件或其他首选的本地行为。如果某项设置必须在 PHI 工作流中不可绕过，应将其写入要求。对于托管默认设置，macOS MDM 托管偏好设置的优先级最高，其次是系统级 <code>managed_config.toml</code>，最后是用户本地的 <code>config.toml</code>。</p> <p>下表汇总了可用于配置 Codex 的部分设置。请结合这些设置和<a href=\"#/article/a-b70d1395449a00f1?anchor=references\">参考资料</a>，按合规需求配置 Codex。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>配置工具</th> <th>可用设置</th> <th>说明</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">登录方式</a></td> <td>ChatGPT 登录；API 密钥登录仅适用于受 BAA 覆盖且启用了 Modified Retention（修改后数据保留）的 API Services。</td> <td>决定适用的是 ChatGPT 工作区控制还是 API 组织控制。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=automatic-approval-reviews\">审批策略</a></td> <td><code>allowed_approval_policies</code> 可设为 <code>\"on-request\"</code>、<code>\"untrusted\"</code>、<code>\"never\"</code>，或使用行内表格配置 <code>granular</code>（细粒度）<sup>1</sup>。</td> <td>配置 Codex 请求审批的时机。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-automatic-review-policy\">审批审查者</a></td> <td><code>allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\", \"auto_review\"]</code></td> <td>配置 Codex 如何处理沙盒边界审批。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">权限配置文件</a><sup>2</sup></td> <td><code>default_permissions = \":workspace\"</code><br>仅允许 <code>:read-only</code> 和 <code>:workspace</code>。</td> <td>此策略允许只读访问和工作区访问，但不允许完全访问。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=web-search-mode\">网页搜索</a></td> <td><code>allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\", \"indexed\", \"live\", \"disabled\"]</code></td> <td>配置 Codex 使用网页的方式。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=pin-feature-flags\">浏览器与计算机操控功能</a></td> <td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td> <td>配置各使用界面对应的功能。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP 服务器</a></td> <td>默认将 <code>[mcp_servers]</code> 留空；仅将身份准确且获准的服务器加入允许列表。</td> <td>默认禁用本地 MCP 服务。仅添加获准的服务器或连接器。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <ol> <li><code>\"granular\"</code> 值允许管理员启用细粒度审批策略。在 <code>allowed_approval_policies</code> 中，应将其写成定义每个审批类别的内联表：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\n     \"on-request\",\n     \"untrusted\",\n     \"never\",\n     { granular = { sandbox_approval = true, rules = true, mcp_elicitations = true, request_permissions = true, skill_approval = true } },\n   ]\n</code></pre> <p>若要在 <code>config.toml</code> 中选择细粒度策略，请将 <code>approval_policy</code> 配置为相同的内联表结构。当某个类别设为 <code>false</code> 时，Codex 会拒绝该类请求，而不是请求批准。</p> <ol> <li>权限配置文件允许列表要求 Codex 版本为 0.138.0 或更高版本。若要强制执行表格中的限制，请在 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中加入完整的允许列表：</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \":read-only\" = true\n   \":workspace\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>如果配置了 <code>[allowed_permission_profiles]</code>，未列出的权限配置文件将被拒绝。因此，不列出 <code>:danger-full-access</code> 就能阻止用户选择完全访问权限。</p> <p>请在 <code>allowed_approval_policies</code> 中保留 <code>untrusted</code>，以继续使用更严格的 Codex 为 <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code> 的项目派生的审批策略。 不要直接设置 <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code>；Codex 和 ChatGPT Work 已不再 支持该设置。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">从已弃用的 <code>untrusted</code> 审批 策略</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"example-1-enable-the-google-drive-app\"></a></p> <p><a id=\"example-1-enable-the-google-drive-plugin\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"1-google-drive\">示例 1：启用 Google Drive 插件</h3> <p>确认数据流、OAuth 权限范围、访问控制和第三方 BAA 状态后，仅为获准的组启用 Google Drive。OpenAI 的 BAA 约束 OpenAI 对 PHI 的处理；它不会自动涵盖作为 PHI 接收方或持有方的 Google。</p> <p>此工作区托管的连接器要求使用 ChatGPT 登录，不能通过 API 密钥进行身份验证。</p> <p>Codex 使用 <code>apps</code> 配置键设置连接器。本示例为 Google Drive 连接器设置本地默认值。请将 <code>&lt;approved-google-drive-app-id&gt;</code> 替换为获准安装项中的准确应用 ID；显示名称或猜测的 ID 无法应用此配置。</p> <blockquote> <p>代码块中的英文注释说明：只有经过法律与安全审查，并获准使用 Google Drive 连接器的用户组，才能采用此配置。请先将占位符替换为获准安装项的准确应用 ID。</p> </blockquote> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Example config.toml change for a group approved to use\n# the Google Drive connector with PHI, after legal and security review.\n[features]\napps = true\n[apps.\"&lt;approved-google-drive-app-id&gt;\"]\nenabled = true\ndestructive_enabled = false\ndefault_tools_approval_mode = \"prompt\"\n</code></pre> <p>这些可由用户配置的默认值会阻止标记为破坏性的连接器工具，并要求审批；除非应用级或单个工具的设置覆盖了这些默认值。它们不是不可绕过的管理员控制措施。请使用工作区和 RBAC 控制访问，并使用托管要求禁用应用或要求对指定的获准工具进行审批。在可用时，请查看 Google Workspace 审计日志，了解连接器活动。</p> <p><a id=\"example-2-use-github-locally\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"2-github\">示例 2：在本地使用 GitHub</h3> <p>许多团队会在开发人员的工作站上使用 Git 或 GitHub CLI 进行本地开发；这与 Codex 云端服务不同。如果代码库、问题、拉取请求或评论中可能包含 PHI，请先确认组织批准在 GitHub 环境中处理此类数据，再启用此方式。</p> <blockquote> <p>代码块中的英文注释说明：以下是本地 GitHub 场景的配置追加项；它不会启用 Codex 云端服务，并会让代码库操作保留可审查环节。</p> </blockquote> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Example requirements.toml addition for local GitHub use.\n# This doesn't enable Codex cloud. It keeps repository actions reviewable.\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before changing repository history.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"gh\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before using GitHub CLI.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>此策略不会阻止使用 GitHub；它会在 Codex 更改代码库历史记录或运行 GitHub CLI 命令前设置审查环节。</p> <p><a id=\"optional-use-a-vetted-github-mcp-server\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"github-mcp\">可选：使用经过审查的 GitHub MCP 服务器</h3> <p>如果团队使用 GitHub MCP 服务器，而不只是本地 Git 命令，请将获准服务器的准确身份加入允许列表，并将工具限制为获准范围内的最小集合。</p> <blockquote> <p>代码块中的英文注释说明：下面的设置是可选项，只有经过审查并获准的 GitHub MCP 服务器才能使用。示例中的服务器地址只是占位内容，必须换成你所在环境获准使用的准确身份和地址。</p> </blockquote> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Optional: allow a vetted GitHub MCP server.\n# Use the exact approved server identity for your environment.\n# requirements.toml\n[mcp_servers.github]\nidentity = { url = \"https://github-mcp.example.com/mcp\" }\n# config.toml\n[mcp_servers.github]\nurl = \"https://github-mcp.example.com/mcp\"\nenabled = true\ndefault_tools_approval_mode = \"prompt\"\nenabled_tools = [\"&lt;approved-read-tools&gt;\", \"&lt;approved-pr-tools&gt;\"]\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"practical-rollout-steps\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">实际部署步骤</h2> <ol> <li><strong>选择获准的登录方式。</strong>确定用户是通过 ChatGPT 登录 <a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Codex Local</a>、使用 API 密钥，还是在不同工作流中分别使用这两种方式。</li> <li><strong>与 OpenAI 确认 BAA。</strong>确认 BAA 涵盖你批准的登录方式。对于 API 密钥工作流，请确认 BAA 将启用修改后数据保留的 API 服务列为合格服务，并且 OpenAI 已为该 API 组织配置修改后数据保留。请联系 OpenAI 客户总监，为 ChatGPT 工作区启用 Codex HIPAA 支持。</li> <li><strong>启用 Codex Local 并定义 RBAC 组。</strong>按照 <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise 管理员设置</a>启用 Codex Local，创建一个范围精简的 Codex 管理员组，并通过 Codex 用户、Codex PHI 用户等 RBAC 组分配 Codex 访问权限。</li> <li><strong>部署管理员强制执行的 <code>requirements.toml</code> 和托管默认设置。</strong>对于受支持的初始策略设置，可使用云端托管要求、MDM 或系统配置。登录限制、工作区固定和 API 密钥工作流则应通过系统配置或 MDM 设置。配置权限配置文件、审批策略、网页搜索模式、功能固定项、网络要求、命令规则和 MCP 允许列表。</li> <li><strong>培训用户了解审批和沙盒边界。</strong>使用<a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">智能体审批与安全</a>说明 Codex 何时可在沙盒内执行操作、何时会请求审批，以及用户为何应审查网络、文件传输、代码库写入和第三方连接器操作。</li> <li><strong>在处理 PHI 前审查第三方插件。</strong>启用包含 Google Drive、GitHub 等连接器、浏览器目标或 MCP 服务器的插件前，请确认组织批准任何会接收 PHI 的第三方，并已与该方签订适当的 BAA。</li> <li><strong>跟踪、审查并更新部署。</strong>使用 Compliance API 导出、工作区分析、端点日志、插件和已连接服务的审计日志，以及代码库审计日志，确认部署状态持续符合内部策略。</li> </ol> <p><a id=\"references\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">参考资料</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Codex 身份验证</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\">Codex 配置基础</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise 管理员设置</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Codex 托管配置</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">智能体审批与安全</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Codex 模型上下文协议</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-05f8d80ccc7b0b64\">Codex 治理</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">Codex 权限</a></li> <li>另请参阅 <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001069-chatgpt-healthcare-and-regulated-workspace-functionality\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT 医疗保健与受监管工作区功能（需联网）</a>。</li> <li>另请参阅 <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001202-chatgpt-for-clinicians\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT for Clinicians（需联网）</a>。</li> <li>另请参阅<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8660679-how-can-i-get-a-business-associate-agreement-baa-with-openai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenAI API 服务的业务伙伴协议（需联网）</a>文章。</li> <li>另请参阅 <a href=\"https://app.safebase.io/portal?itemUid=382f924d-54f3-43a8-a9df-c39e6c959958&amp;source=search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex 安全白皮书（需联网）</a>。</li> </ul>","en":"<h1 id=\"hipaa-configuration-guide-for-codex\">HIPAA configuration guide for Codex</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"whos-this-for\">Who's this for</h2> <p>This guide is for IT administrators and compliance professionals to get familiar with the shared responsibility for managing Protected Health Information (PHI) in Codex. Codex includes the ChatGPT desktop app, Codex IDE extension, and Codex CLI, which run on your users' computers. It does not include using Codex in the cloud.</p> <p>If you use ChatGPT for Healthcare, ChatGPT for Clinicians, or a Regulated workspace, have an applicable OpenAI Business Associate Agreement (BAA), and have the required Codex access, OpenAI handles PHI it receives from Codex consistent with the BAA. OpenAI securely handles prompts, files, and other inputs it receives through your use of Codex and securely returns outputs to you.</p> <p>OpenAI and your organization share responsibility for securing OpenAI services. You are responsible for securely configuring local workstations, source repositories, local retention, local MCP servers, Browser Use and Computer Use activity, desktop apps, and third-party services such as plugins and apps.</p> <p>OpenAI provides resources to help you configure Codex, including <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/running-codex-safely/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">our web page (online)</a>, <a href=\"#/article/a-f05fc21d24fe24c1\">documentation</a>, and the <a href=\"https://trust.openai.com/?itemUid=382f924d-54f3-43a8-a9df-c39e6c959958&amp;source=click\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex Security Whitepaper (online)</a> on the Trust Portal. Review this material before allowing Codex to be used with PHI. This guide provides examples of how some of these tools can be used.</p> <p>This guide isn't legal advice. If you're a covered entity or business associate, review the final configuration against your HIPAA policies, risk analysis, BAA inventory, endpoint controls, and data-flow documentation.</p> <h2 id=\"shared-responsibility\">Shared responsibility</h2> <p>As with most cloud solutions, the cloud service provider and customer share compliance responsibility. ChatGPT Enterprise stores inputs and outputs in the OpenAI cloud. Your users' workstations keep inputs and outputs from Codex. Codex sends inputs, such as prompts and files, to OpenAI for inference, and OpenAI returns the outputs. For usage authenticated through ChatGPT, OpenAI keeps audit records for up to 30 days so you can retrieve them through the <a href=\"#/article/a-05f8d80ccc7b0b64?anchor=compliance-api\">Compliance API</a>. OpenAI doesn't train on ChatGPT Enterprise data or Codex data.</p> <p>Your local workstation configuration, specifically its TOML policy files, governs what Codex can do on a user's machine. It affects whether Codex can read and write files, run commands, use network access, invoke plugins or connectors, call MCP tools, open browser surfaces, and keep local transcripts.</p> <p>These settings don't change OpenAI's BAA obligations, but they're central to your HIPAA safeguards. You are responsible for configuring these settings consistent with your PHI handling policies.</p> <h2 id=\"openai-security-program\">OpenAI security program</h2> <p>OpenAI maintains an enterprise security program designed to protect the data processed by OpenAI services and to support regulated organizations in meeting their compliance obligations. More information on OpenAI's security program can be found at the <a href=\"https://trust.openai.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Trust Portal (online)</a>.</p> <p>OpenAI deploys an <strong>Enterprise Risk Management</strong> program and a formal risk governance structure that includes reporting to board committees. Product assurance activities help ensure product launches preserve safeguards such as encryption, least-privileged access, and granular logging to support HIPAA compliance. Product risk assessments, control monitoring, and compliance reviews help identify reasonably anticipated risks to your data, evaluate the effectiveness of safeguards, and support continuous improvement of controls used by ChatGPT Enterprise, the API platform, and Codex-related services.</p> <p><strong>Secure development and CI/CD safeguards</strong> help reduce the risk that changes to Codex-related services introduce unauthorized access, data leakage, or integrity issues. These safeguards include controlled source access, peer review, automated testing, security checks in build and deployment workflows, secret-handling controls, and monitored deployment processes. A controlled software-delivery process supports the OpenAI service layer, while you're still responsible for local repository hygiene, workstation security, and the behavior permitted by local policy-file configuration.</p> <p><strong>OpenAI's vulnerability management program</strong> includes continuous scans, dependency and infrastructure reviews, severity-based triage, remediation tracking, and validation of fixes. OpenAI also uses internal and external red teams, independent security testing, and responsible-disclosure channels to identify and address security weaknesses before they can affect your data.</p> <p><strong>Data protection controls</strong> include encryption of your data in transit and at rest, identity and access controls, role-based administration, logging, and retention controls that follow the applicable ChatGPT Enterprise or API organization settings.</p> <h2 id=\"codex-sign-in\">Codex sign-in</h2> <p>Codex supports two OpenAI sign-in methods when using OpenAI models: ChatGPT sign-in for subscription access and API key sign-in for usage-based access. ChatGPT sign-in requires a HIPAA-eligible account, the applicable OpenAI BAA, and the required Codex access and workspace permissions. For API key sign-in, the BAA covers data processed by OpenAI only if it includes API Services with Modified Retention as an Eligible Service. OpenAI must also provision the API organization with Modified Retention unless it specifies otherwise. See <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001069-hipaa-eligible-products-and-functionality\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">HIPAA Eligible Products and Functionality (online)</a> for more information. You are responsible for completing a BAA with any third party that may access PHI through any installed plugin or app.</p> <p>With ChatGPT sign-in, Codex usage follows the user's ChatGPT workspace permissions, role-based access control (RBAC), and ChatGPT Enterprise retention and residency settings. With API key sign-in, Codex usage follows the OpenAI API organization's retention, data-sharing, and administrative settings rather than the ChatGPT workspace settings. API key sign-in is commonly used for programmatic Codex CLI workflows, such as trusted CI/CD jobs, but you shouldn't expose API keys in public or untrusted execution environments.</p> <h2 id=\"your-responsibilities\">Your responsibilities</h2> <p>You remain responsible for the workstations where Codex runs. Complete your own risk analysis for local Codex use, including controls such as workstation configuration, operating system security, disk encryption, malware protection, device management, patching, user access, secure credential storage, and local retention.</p> <p>You decide which users may use Codex, which sign-in methods they can use, which workspaces they can access, whether they can sign in with an API key, which repositories and folders can contain PHI, and whether Codex may use external services.</p> <p>You're also responsible for enabled third-party services and which users have access to them. If your organization enables a browser destination, plugin, connector, or MCP server for Microsoft SharePoint, Google Drive, GitHub, or another service in an environment with PHI, confirm that your organization approves the service for PHI and has an appropriate BAA or comparable healthcare addendum. OpenAI's BAA doesn't make another vendor a HIPAA-compliant destination.</p> <p>The following sections explain how you can use the <code>requirements.toml</code> policy configuration file and related settings to control how Codex operates. Review the <a href=\"#/article/a-f05fc21d24fe24c1\">OpenAI documentation</a> for other settings, and revisit that review periodically as Codex capabilities change.</p> <h2 id=\"enable-codex\">Enable Codex</h2> <p>Follow the <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise admin setup instructions</a> to enable Codex Local for your workspace and confirm that users have the required permissions and that you have a BAA with OpenAI.</p> <p><strong>The BAA doesn't cover Codex cloud. Don't use Codex cloud with PHI.</strong></p> <h2 id=\"configure-role-based-access-control\">Configure role-based access control</h2> <p>You can customize access to Codex and its configuration by using RBAC. For example, users who don't interact with PHI may receive a more permissive configuration, while users who do interact with PHI may receive the configuration in this guide. Control organization-wide access to Codex from the <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/permissions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT admin permissions and roles page (online)</a>. To control access for specific users, create groups and edit the permissions for those groups.</p> <p><a id=\"review-apps-and-plugins\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"review-plugins-and-connectors\">Review plugins and connectors</h2> <p>Codex in the ChatGPT desktop app and Codex CLI support plugins, which can include connectors and skills. Plugins aren't available in the IDE extension. Connectors let you exchange data with third-party data sources. Before enabling a plugin with a connector, determine whether you need a BAA with any third party that receives data through the connector. Skills are instructions that operate within the policy configuration. Review skills to make sure they're fit for purpose, as you would any other script.</p> <p>Workspace admins must make a plugin available through plugin controls and separately enable its connectors before users can use them. Configure connector access in <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/admin/ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">connector settings (online)</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-managed-requirements-and-defaults\">Configure managed requirements and defaults</h2> <p>Requirements and managed defaults in TOML configuration files manage Codex behavior. To set admin-enforced constraints that users can't override, use managed requirements in <code>requirements.toml</code>. OpenAI recommends using managed configuration to enforce your data-handling requirements for PHI.</p> <p>Admins can configure cloud-managed requirements on the <a href=\"https://chatgpt.com/codex/settings/managed-configs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex managed configuration page (online)</a> by using <code>requirements.toml</code>-compatible syntax. They can also distribute requirements through device management such as macOS MDM. Codex applies requirements from lower to higher precedence: system <code>requirements.toml</code>, cloud-managed requirements, legacy <code>managed_config.toml</code> requirements, and macOS MDM requirements. Higher-precedence layers override ordinary scalar and list values; some requirements have field-specific merge behavior.</p> <p>To restrict PHI workflows to an approved ChatGPT workspace, deploy both <code>allowed_login_methods = [\"chatgpt\"]</code> and <code>allowed_chatgpt_workspaces = [\"&lt;workspace-id&gt;\"]</code> through system <code>requirements.toml</code> or MDM. Cloud-managed requirements ignore both settings, and workspace restrictions alone don't block API key sign-in. API key workflows also require system or MDM requirements because they don't receive workspace cloud-managed requirements.</p> <p>Managed defaults are separate from requirements. They set the initial configuration that Codex starts with, but users can change those settings during a session. Codex reapplies the defaults the next time it starts. Use managed defaults for standardization, not strict compliance enforcement. For example, you might set a default model, permission profile, or other preferred local behavior. If a setting must be non-bypassable for PHI workflows, put it in requirements instead. For managed defaults, macOS MDM managed preferences have the highest precedence, followed by system <code>managed_config.toml</code> and then the user's local <code>config.toml</code>.</p> <p>The following table summarizes some settings available for configuring Codex. Review these settings and the resources in <a href=\"#/article/a-b70d1395449a00f1?anchor=references\">References</a> to configure Codex in a way that aligns with your compliance needs.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Configuration tool</th> <th>Available settings</th> <th>Explanation</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Sign-in method</a></td> <td>ChatGPT sign-in; API key sign-in only with BAA-covered API Services with Modified Retention.</td> <td>Determines whether ChatGPT workspace controls or API organization controls apply.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=automatic-approval-reviews\">Approval policy</a></td> <td><code>allowed_approval_policies</code>: <code>\"on-request\"</code>, <code>\"untrusted\"</code>, <code>\"never\"</code>, and inline-table <code>granular</code><sup>1</sup></td> <td>Configures when Codex requests approval.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-automatic-review-policy\">Approval reviewer</a></td> <td><code>allowed_approvals_reviewers = [\"user\", \"auto_review\"]</code></td> <td>Configures how Codex routes sandbox boundary approvals.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-available-permission-profiles\">Permission profiles</a><sup>2</sup></td> <td><code>default_permissions = \":workspace\"</code><br>Allow only <code>:read-only</code> and <code>:workspace</code>.</td> <td>This policy allows read-only and workspace access, but not full access.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76?anchor=web-search-mode\">Web search</a></td> <td><code>allowed_web_search_modes = [\"cached\", \"indexed\", \"live\", \"disabled\"]</code></td> <td>Configures how Codex uses the web.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=pin-feature-flags\">Browser and computer-use features</a></td> <td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td> <td>Configures surface-specific features.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">MCP servers</a></td> <td>Leave <code>[mcp_servers]</code> empty by default; allowlist only exact, approved servers.</td> <td>Disables local MCP services by default. Add only approved servers or connectors.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <ol> <li>The <code>\"granular\"</code> value allows administrators to permit granular approval policies. In <code>allowed_approval_policies</code>, encode it as an inline table that defines every approval category:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   allowed_approval_policies = [\n     \"on-request\",\n     \"untrusted\",\n     \"never\",\n     { granular = { sandbox_approval = true, rules = true, mcp_elicitations = true, request_permissions = true, skill_approval = true } },\n   ]\n</code></pre> <p>To select a granular policy in <code>config.toml</code>, configure <code>approval_policy</code> with the same inline-table shape. When a category is <code>false</code>, Codex rejects those requests instead of asking for approval.</p> <ol> <li>Permission-profile allowlists require Codex 0.138.0 or later. To enforce the restriction in the table, include the complete allowlist in <code>requirements.toml</code>:</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\">   default_permissions = \":workspace\"\n\n   [allowed_permission_profiles]\n   \":read-only\" = true\n   \":workspace\" = true\n</code></pre> <p>When <code>[allowed_permission_profiles]</code> is present, omitted profiles are denied. Omitting <code>:danger-full-access</code> therefore prevents users from selecting full access.</p> <p>Keep <code>untrusted</code> in <code>allowed_approval_policies</code> to preserve the stricter approval policy Codex derives for projects with <code>trust_level = \"untrusted\"</code>. Don't set <code>approval_policy = \"untrusted\"</code> directly; Codex and ChatGPT Work no longer support that setting. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3?anchor=migrate-from-the-retired-untrusted-approval-policy\">Migrate from the retired <code>untrusted</code> approval policy</a>.</p> <p><a id=\"example-1-enable-the-google-drive-app\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"example-1-enable-the-google-drive-plugin\">Example 1: Enable the Google Drive plugin</h3> <p>Enable Google Drive only for an approved group after confirming its data flow, OAuth scopes, access controls, and third-party BAA posture. OpenAI's BAA governs OpenAI's handling of PHI; it doesn't automatically cover Google as a recipient or holder of PHI.</p> <p>This workspace-managed connector requires ChatGPT sign-in and isn't available with API key authentication.</p> <p>Codex uses the <code>apps</code> configuration key for connector settings. This example sets local defaults for the Google Drive connector. Replace <code>&lt;approved-google-drive-app-id&gt;</code> with the exact app ID from your approved installation; a display name or guessed ID won't apply the configuration.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Example config.toml change for a group approved to use\n# the Google Drive connector with PHI, after legal and security review.\n[features]\napps = true\n[apps.\"&lt;approved-google-drive-app-id&gt;\"]\nenabled = true\ndestructive_enabled = false\ndefault_tools_approval_mode = \"prompt\"\n</code></pre> <p>These user-configurable defaults block connector tools marked as destructive and request approval unless app-level or per-tool settings override them. They aren't non-bypassable admin controls. Use workspace and RBAC controls to restrict access, and use managed requirements to disable an app or require approval for specific approved tools. Review Google Workspace audit logs for connector activity where available.</p> <h3 id=\"example-2-use-github-locally\">Example 2: Use GitHub locally</h3> <p>For local development, many teams use Git or the GitHub CLI from the developer workstation. This is different from Codex cloud. If repositories, issues, pull requests, or comments can contain PHI, confirm that your organization approves the GitHub environment for that data before enabling this path.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Example requirements.toml addition for local GitHub use.\n# This doesn't enable Codex cloud. It keeps repository actions reviewable.\n[rules]\nprefix_rules = [\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"git\" }, { any_of = [\"push\", \"commit\"] }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before changing repository history.\" },\n  { pattern = [{ token = \"gh\" }], decision = \"prompt\", justification = \"Require review before using GitHub CLI.\" },\n]\n</code></pre> <p>This policy doesn't block GitHub use. It creates a review point before Codex changes repository history or uses GitHub CLI commands.</p> <h3 id=\"optional-use-a-vetted-github-mcp-server\">Optional: Use a vetted GitHub MCP server</h3> <p>If your team uses a GitHub MCP server instead of only local Git commands, allowlist the exact approved server identity and restrict tools to the smallest approved set.</p> <pre><code class=\"language-toml\"># Optional: allow a vetted GitHub MCP server.\n# Use the exact approved server identity for your environment.\n# requirements.toml\n[mcp_servers.github]\nidentity = { url = \"https://github-mcp.example.com/mcp\" }\n# config.toml\n[mcp_servers.github]\nurl = \"https://github-mcp.example.com/mcp\"\nenabled = true\ndefault_tools_approval_mode = \"prompt\"\nenabled_tools = [\"&lt;approved-read-tools&gt;\", \"&lt;approved-pr-tools&gt;\"]\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"practical-rollout-steps\">Practical rollout steps</h2> <ol> <li><strong>Select the approved sign-in path.</strong> Decide whether users will <a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">authenticate to Codex Local</a> with ChatGPT, use API keys, or use both for separate workflows.</li> <li><strong>Confirm the BAA with OpenAI.</strong> Confirm that your BAA covers your approved sign-in paths. For API key workflows, confirm that it includes API Services with Modified Retention as an Eligible Service and that OpenAI has provisioned the API organization with Modified Retention. Contact your OpenAI Account Director to enable Codex HIPAA support for a ChatGPT workspace.</li> <li><strong>Enable Codex Local and define RBAC groups.</strong> Use <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise admin setup</a> to enable Codex Local, create a small Codex Admin group, and assign Codex access through RBAC groups such as Codex Users and Codex PHI Users.</li> <li><strong>Deploy admin-enforced <code>requirements.toml</code> and managed defaults.</strong> Use cloud-managed requirements, MDM, or system configuration for supported starter-policy settings. Use system configuration or MDM for sign-in restrictions, workspace pinning, and API key workflows. Configure permission profiles, approval policies, web search modes, feature pins, network requirements, command rules, and MCP allowlists.</li> <li><strong>Train users on approvals and sandbox boundaries.</strong> Use <a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">Agent approvals and security</a> to explain when Codex can act inside the sandbox, when it asks for approval, and why users should review network, file-transfer, repository-write, and third-party connector actions.</li> <li><strong>Review third-party plugins before PHI use.</strong> Before enabling plugins with connectors such as Google Drive and GitHub, browser destinations, or MCP servers, confirm that your organization approves any third party receiving PHI and has an appropriate BAA with that party.</li> <li><strong>Track, review, and refresh the deployment.</strong> Use Compliance API exports, workspace analytics, endpoint logs, plugin and connected-service audit logs, and repository audit logs to confirm that the deployed posture stays aligned with your internal policies.</li> </ol> <h2 id=\"references\">References</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-1454a3aad1fa2115\">Codex authentication</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-d684e38d0094ab76\">Codex config basics</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4\">Codex Enterprise admin setup</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Codex managed configuration</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-b7af4e0dbfe643b3\">Agent approvals and security</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-dd3c07a3ae6aafeb\">Codex Model Context Protocol</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-05f8d80ccc7b0b64\">Codex governance</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-4d7a5dc78b3c591e\">Codex permissions</a></li> <li>See <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001069-chatgpt-healthcare-and-regulated-workspace-functionality\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Healthcare and Regulated Workspace functionality (online)</a>.</li> <li>See <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001202-chatgpt-for-clinicians\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT for Clinicians (online)</a>.</li> <li>See the <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8660679-how-can-i-get-a-business-associate-agreement-baa-with-openai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Business Associate Agreement for OpenAI API services (online)</a> article.</li> <li>See the <a href=\"https://app.safebase.io/portal?itemUid=382f924d-54f3-43a8-a9df-c39e6c959958&amp;source=search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Codex Security Whitepaper (online)</a>.</li> </ul>"};
